UNSHAR                         OS-9/68000                         UNSHARNAME     unshar -- unpack shell archives from news and mailSYNOPSIS     unshar [-c directory] [-d directory] [-h file] [-f] [-n] [-s] [file ...]DESCRIPTION     A shell archive -- a "shar" -- is how programs were posted to Usenet:     a script that, run by the Unix shell, writes out the files it carries.     unshar unpacks one without a Unix shell.  It skips the mail or news     header in front of the archive and runs the rest through its own small     interpreter, which knows the commands shell archives use: cat and sed     writing here-documents, test, if, case and for, echo, mkdir and cd.       $ unshar which6.shar       unshar:  Doing which6.shar:       Extracting which6.c       Extracting which.1       Extracting Makefile     With no file named, it reads the archive from standard input.  The     files are written in the current data directory.     A line it cannot carry out is shown with "You'll have to do this     yourself" -- chmod, most often, which has no meaning here -- and the     rest of the archive is still unpacked.  Archives that check each     file's size after writing it will say so if one comes out wrong.OPTIONS     -c directory, -d directory          change to the directory first, making it if it does not exist.          A name beginning with ~ is taken from $HOME; a name beginning          with ? is asked for.     -f   unpack even if the input does not look like a shell archive     -h file          append the headers to this file     -s   save the headers in <file>.hdr, or UNSHAR.HDR for standard input     -n   do not save the headersENVIRONMENT     UNSHARDIR  the directory for -c, when none is given     HOME       used for a directory beginning with ~NOTES     An archive has to have OS-9's line endings, CR, to be read a line at     a time.  One brought over with LF endings arrives as a single long     line; autolf converts it.     The interpreter knows the forms shell archives are written in, not the     whole shell.  An archive that does something cleverer is shown the line     it stopped at.     A sample archive is here: DOC/unshar/which6.shar, the posting that     which(1) on this disk came from.ORIGIN     cshar 2.0, patchlevel 3 -- Rich Salz's tools to create and unpack shell     archives, comp.sources.unix volume 15, 1988.  Released into the public     domain: "do what you want, but let your conscience be your guide."     SRC/cshar has the source; SRC/cshar/README.OSK says what the OS-9     version changes, including two fixes to the interpreter.